From 2a35439ef19f65919a00f8582e646342b75a4421 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:08:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: clustered shell -o values,
 --rcfile, external time; one keyword walk

PR #2191 review nits (review 5891136534), both non-blocking.

1) `_shell_script_operand` skipped `-o`/`-O`/`+o`/`+O` values only as standalone tokens, so
   `bash -euo pipefail /x/hermes-sync`, `bash -eo pipefail ...` and `bash --rcfile FILE ...`
   took the option value as the script and dropped the hermes-sync hint. Each `o`/`O` in a
   short-option cluster now consumes the next token, `--rcfile`/`--init-file` consume their
   value, other `--long` options are skipped, and a lone `-` ends options like `--`.
   The referenced-script walk (refusal path) had its own copy of the same loop; it now uses the
   helper too, so `bash -euo pipefail ~/deploy.sh` is scanned instead of resolving `pipefail`.
   To keep that walk fail closed, a `--rcfile`/`--init-file` value is still scanned as a file
   the shell may source (`bash --rcfile x.sh -i`).

2) `_COMMAND_PREFIX_KEYWORDS`/`_peel_command_keywords` duplicated the `_SUBSHELL_OPENING_WORDS`
   walk in `_opens_call_arguments` and differed (no `coproc`; `time` options peeled in any
   order). Both now use one `_peel_reserved_words` over `_RESERVED_PREFIX_WORDS`, with bash's
   order `time [-p] [--]`, `function NAME`, and `coproc NAME` only before a compound command
   (`coproc cmd args` runs cmd). External time(1) after an assignment or wrapper
   (`x=1 time hermes-sync`, `env time -f %e hermes-sync`) is peeled in `executes_command_named`
   by `_peel_external_time` (its `-f`/`-o` values consumed, wrappers after it re-peeled).
   `_TRANSPARENT_COMMAND_PREFIXES` is unchanged, so refusal paths and shell_heredoc are not
   widened by this.

Tests: tests/tools/test_lifecycle_guard_hermes_sync_hint.py gains end-to-end refusal hint
cases (clustered -o, +eo, --rcfile, --init-file), _refusal_hint cases (x=1 time, env time -f,
coproc), executes_command_named cases (including `time -- -p hermes-sync` -> False, which the
old any-order peel answered True) and a fail-closed check that `bash -euo pipefail deploy.sh`
and `bash --rcfile deploy.sh -i` stay refused without the hint.
RED on main's lifecycle_guard.py: 21 of the new cases fail; GREEN: 58/58.
All 22 test files importing lifecycle_guard / terminal_tool_guards / shell_heredoc: 1461 passed.
scripts/check --base origin/main: 19 checks ok.

Rebased over 8ccca2606f (output-sink echo/printf walk) and 2e73a605a8
(programs fed on stdin): the echo/printf segment walk peels via the shared
`_peel_reserved_words`; the shell branch of `_references_at` keeps
2e73a605a8's stdin handling (`-s`, no operand, `< f`, `cat f |`) but reads
the option walk from one `_shell_options_end` helper (also behind
`_shell_script_operand`), so `bash -euo pipefail < f.sh`,
`bash -euo pipefail -s < f.sh` and `cat f.sh | bash -eo pipefail` now scan
f.sh (pinned in test_lifecycle_guard_interpreter_runners.py: RED on main 3
failed, GREEN 61 passed).
